nodecode-tls-posture is a rewrite of Pacific-Cloud-Solutions's Pacific-Cloud-Solutions/hermes-plugins:

  https://github.com/Pacific-Cloud-Solutions/hermes-plugins
  commit 389d300cd43de459fd6f260498ba3c2e7fff95dc
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Pacific Cloud Solutions (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the one model-facing tool, tls_posture, with its three arguments
(a tree to audit, include_info, expiry_days); the fixed search paths it
reads (etc/ssl/certs, etc/pki/tls/certs, the Let's Encrypt live
directories, and the nginx and Apache configuration); the twelve checks
TLS-001 .. TLS-012; the join between a certificate file and the server
block that presents it, including the fullchain.pem sibling rule and
Apache's separate chain directive; the two reading rules the original is
careful about (a cipher string is a list of offers and exclusions, and
nginx writes the protocol versions it enables while Apache writes the ones
it removes, compared as whole tokens); the report shape (tool, target,
grade, verdict, by-severity counts, findings with evidence, and a coverage
block naming what ran, what was skipped and why); and the /tls command and
the manual topic, which the original had no equivalent of. The Python is
Common Lisp here: nlk:define-contract carries the tool's arguments, and the
X.509 reader is one small DER walk in x509.lisp, so the cell needs no
dependency beyond Nodecode itself.

Left out: Python's `cryptography` and the stdlib `ssl` fallback, and with
them the honest degradation between them — one decoder here always answers
validity, names, key size and signature digest, so TLS-004 and TLS-005 are
never skipped for want of a library. Also left out: the shared
pcs-security-core contract and its taint/provenance model, replaced by the
report shape above with the untrusted text kept in the evidence field; the
host-level registrations core owns (the system-prompt section that says
evidence is data, and the extra secret-redaction patterns), since a cell
registers what it declares; and the plugin's install-scanner concerns
(manifest keys, hostile-vocabulary lists), which have no counterpart here.
